<DocsLayout
  title="Updating the plugin — Apple Docs"
  description="Roblox never updates Studio plugins for you. How Apple tells you an update exists, how to apply it, and why running an old build is normal rather than broken."
  heading="Updating the plugin"
>
  <p>
    Roblox does not update Studio plugins automatically. There is no silent background update and
    nothing happens on restart — a person has to press a button. That is platform behaviour, not a
    Apple choice, and it is why this page exists at all.
  </p>

  <h2>How to update</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      In Studio, open the plugin management window — Roblox's documentation calls it
      <strong>Plugin Management</strong>; the <strong>Plugins</strong> tab opens it via
      <strong>Manage Plugins</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>Find <strong>Apple</strong> and press its <strong>Update</strong> button.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    There is also an <strong>Update all Plugins</strong> button. Roblox staff confirmed in April
    2026 that the bulk button was broken and a fix was coming, and that the individual
    <strong>Update</strong> buttons still work. If the bulk button does nothing, use the per-plugin
    one.
  </p>

  <h2>How you'll know</h2>
  <p>
    You do not have to check. The plugin tells the server which build it is on every time it polls,
    and the server answers.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>An amber line in the panel</strong> when a newer build is published — for example
      <em>“Apple 1.1.0 is available. Update it in Studio → Plugins → Manage Plugins → Update.”</em>
      It is also written to Studio's Output window <em>once</em> per message, not on every poll.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A red line</strong> in the rare case where a build is genuinely too old for the server
      to work with. That one blocks builds and is not advisory.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Silence</strong> the rest of the time, which is the normal state.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Which build am I on?</h2>
  <p>
    The Apple panel prints it at the bottom, as <code>Apple Studio · 1.0.0 · independent preview</code>. The web
    workspace shows the same version under <strong>Studio</strong> in the project rail once the
    plugin has reported in. What changed between builds is on the
    <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two numbers, because they answer different questions. <strong>Version</strong> is the build you
    have, and it moves on every release including cosmetic ones. <strong>Protocol</strong> is the
    contract the plugin and the server speak, and it almost never moves.
  </p>

  <h2>Running an old build is fine</h2>
  <p>
    Apple's server decides whether to talk to a plugin by <em>protocol</em>, never by version. A
    plugin several releases behind is served normally as long as it still speaks a contract the
    server supports — and today the server supports every protocol that has ever shipped, so nothing
    in the wild is refused.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is deliberate. Because updates require you to notice a message and click a button in a
    different window, a server that refused old clients would strand people with no self-service way
    out. Version skew is the expected steady state here, not an error to be stamped out. What you
    miss by staying behind is new capability and fixes, not access.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>If an update drops the connection.</strong> Your project link lives in the plugin's
      own saved settings rather than in the build, so it normally survives an update. If the panel
      comes back as <strong>Not connected</strong>, nothing is wrong and nothing is lost —
      <a href="/docs/connect">connect again</a> with a new code.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
